during my time at university i completed  quite a few degrees i started enrolling  myself in a bachelor of philosophy with  minors in history in arts and in  economics next i wrote myself in a  bachelor of business administration both  at the university of munich lmu actually  completed this one in five semesters the  bachelor philosophy in the regular six  semesters during my bachelor and  business administration i also spent a  semester abroad at bocconi university in  milan  then during the middle of my bachelor i  enrolled myself in a bachelor of laws at  the distance university haagen in the  later completed that as well at the same  time i wrote myself at the cdtm program  which is a joint institution of the tu  munich the technical university munich  and the lmu munich and i graduated with  an honors degree in technology  management i then later enrolled for  master in accounting and finance at  london school of economics and then of  course in the end i also completed my  mba at insead so in this video i'm going  to talk about exactly that how did i do  that how did i study three bachelor  degrees in parallel and i will also  share a couple of tips if you intend to  do something similar as well and the  last will reflect whether this actually  helped myself in my career or not so  let's get out and let's get going it's a  beautiful weekend here in munich today  we're visiting some of the munich lakes  so let me share a couple of  considerations that you have in your  mind when thinking about starting  several bachelors at the same time the  first is the selection of the university  here especially some private business  schools might not even offer different  degree programs in many private business  schools you can indeed only study  business the second limitation is that  often also the regulations of university  sometimes do not allow you to be  enrolled in several degree programs at  the same time here in germany it's  especially the big public universities  that often a little bit more lenient i  studied at the university of munich for  my first two bachelor degrees in the  third distance university so make sure  that the choice of university supports  the study programs that you want to  study indeed second make sure to combine  subjects that actually compatible with  each other in regards to the examination  structure that they have here one  advantage of comparing for instance a  business degree with liberal arts is  that business studies usually are very  heavy on exams so at the end of the  semester you will usually have an exam  period where i had lots of exams and on  the other hand for liberal arts there  are usually almost no exams at all that  examination structure looks the way that  you will write lots of essays lots of  written course work that you need to  prepare and these will often need to be  written during the semester so i did the  advantage that at the end of the  semester i was able to just study for my  business exams and didn't need to do  that much work during the semester for  this degree and during the semester then  and indeed had the time to focus for the  philosophy degree and make that work  third thing strategically about the  semester abroad that you might do so i  did a semester abroad at bocconi  university in milan and that actually  helped me to finish my business degree  earlier because i indeed finished my  bachelor in business in five semesters  instead of six semesters including the  bachelor thieves and one of the main  ways i did this is that in milan and  bocconi i wrote a full exam period and i  was able to carry over many of the exams  that i wrote in milan towards my  business degree and then once i came  back from milan i also completed a full  exam period in the same semester in  munich as well because there the exams  happen a little bit later than the exams  in milan and still a time couple of  weeks to prepare for these exams so  pretty much did the course work of two  semesters and only one semester and this  that enabled me to for instance in my  last semester complete an internship at  mckinsey this enabled me then to fully  focus on my bachelor thieves in  philosophy and semester number six many  students actually extend their studies  if they do a semester abroad in my  experience if you set it up the right  way can actually help you to complete  your studies in a quicker way let's now  talk about time management because this  is a topic i quite often get questions  on how did you manage to do all this how  did you manage your time in the past  already created a video about this topic  i will link it somewhere above here let  me also know in the comments if you  would like to have more content on that  i think the basic insight is the time  management in these types of situations  isn't even that challenging if you just  have the pressure that on this day you  will have the exam you will make sure  that the day before the exams you will  do the study if there is a course work  and essay that you need to submit you  will make sure that in the days before  you will write this essay because what  would you do instead and the truth is  most people would just in the evenings  watch netflix hang out maybe with some  friends and so on which of course can  also be fun but personally i found that  i can still do lots of these things i  still had lots of fun was going out with  friends but a couple of asses every  semester a couple of exams in the end of  the semester this is still very much  possible if you just have the deadline  the pressure another smallest force to  put in the time to not fail so was all  the effort really worth it let's look at  that from three perspectives first to  get my very first job in consulting at  mckinsey second for my mid to long term  career development and third just for my  very personal life first for mckinsey  did this help me to get into mckinsey  and very frankly i'd say yes it did help  me i think it was something a little bit  special that i did to study both  business philosophy law surely not all  the other business candidates did that i  think especially when you study business  it's important to have a special spike  something special that other people are  not necessarily doing for me i'd say  that was one of the factors of course do  not know how my profile would have been  assessed if i wouldn't have done that  but for sure that did not hurt me second  how did this help me with my roads after  mckinsey and you frankly i didn't think  it had that much of an effort more or  less all the things i did after mckinsey  were very much based on my network  personal relationships that i had up to  this job frankly i'm not sure if i will  ever apply to any job again in the  future wherever just classically with a  cv in a code way apply somewhere i think  that's probably true independent from  your degree i think the further you  advance in your career the less  important your actual degrees will be  that you may be accomplished attained  somewhere in the past i think at some  point it's just not that important  anymore i think it especially helps you  to get in to get that very first job and  third how do i assess this for myself  for my personal development for the  knowledge that i built and here frankly  starting with the business degree  spending a couple of years in management  consulting will be at least as effective  at starting completing a batchline  business so that's why it helped me of  course to get the context understand a  couple of things a bit better just for  that probably the timing consulting  alone would have done at least that much  for me myself second the philosophy  degree i think this very much shaped  just how i approach problems how i think  about problems i think it's just very  difficult also for myself to see how i  would approach things so i would see  things if i wouldn't have studied that  degree surely it helped me a lot to  develop my critical thinking it's very  difficult for me to assess how this  would have looked like if i wouldn't  have done that and third the law degree  this might be actually the degree where  i got the most practical value from  especially my roles after consulting  because directly after mckinsey i moved  into a mid-sized german retailer in a  restructuring role as a cto and i was  faced with lots of legal questions in  this position i did work together with  many lawyers at the time here this legal  basic understanding was insanely  important helpful to me and even now in  my current role in startup in the  management position in startup i'm also  facing level questions almost all the  time and it's really important helpful  for me to have this background of course  i'm not as qualified as a lawyer but at  least i know what they're talking about  i can assess whether what someone tells  me makes sense or not and this is  immensely helpful indeed so did you  study several degree programs in  parallel as well let me know about your  experiences in the comments i do hope  this maybe inspired some of you to do  similar things in the future as well if  you took away any value out of this  video at all please destroy the like  button for the youtube algorithm and  also subscribe and turn on the  notification bell to stay up to date  with the content big big thanks to all  the members of the channel for your help  this is very much appreciated my name is  heineke already content every single  saturday here on the video so all the  best to you see you next week and bye

